{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, Mr. ___ . I'm Dr. ___ . You came in today with chest pain. Can you tell me what happened?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, doctor. This morning I felt a little chest pressure, but it went away. I went to my dialysis like usual and felt fine after."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:32:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Then I went for a walk, and the pain came back—left side, sharp, and it spread to my lower back. I also felt nauseous."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:33:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And did you take your nitroglycerin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:34:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, I wasn’t at home, so I didn’t have it with me. I walked to the ER instead. They gave me one there, and it helped right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. That’s a good sign. Your EKG didn’t show any new changes, and we’ve checked your heart enzymes three times."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They’re stable—no rise in troponin. That means no heart attack this time. It looks like your usual angina, triggered by activity."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not serious? But it felt just like before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s important, but not an emergency. Your heart muscle wasn’t damaged. We’ll keep you overnight to monitor you and make sure it doesn’t come back."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Should I be worried about walking now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You can still be active, but listen to your body. If you feel chest pain, stop and rest. Use your nitroglycerin if it doesn’t go away in 5 minutes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:41:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We also reviewed your meds. We’re increasing your amlodipine from 2.5 mg to 5 mg daily to help prevent these episodes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:42:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that safe with my blood pressure? It runs low sometimes."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2136-02-27 18:43:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor it. Your blood pressure today is 139 over 58, which is acceptable. Amlodipine helps relax your blood vessels and reduce the heart’s workload."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. You’ve had a quiet night—no chest pain, and your vitals are stable. We’re planning to send you home today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good to hear. Should I change anything else besides the amlodipine?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No. Keep taking all your other medications as before—especially your aspirin, metoprolol, and nitroglycerin as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Weigh yourself every morning. If you gain more than three pounds in a day, call your doctor—it could mean fluid buildup."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And my dialysis—should I go as scheduled?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue your regular dialysis. Your fistula looks good—no issues there."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my insulin? I’m very sensitive to it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We reduced your long-acting insulin from 12 to 6 units daily. That’s because you’re on dialysis, and your sugar can drop. Monitor closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2136-02-28 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, doctor. I’ll be careful."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2136-02-28 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your discharge papers are ready. You’re going home with a diagnosis of stable angina—no new heart damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2136-02-28 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep your cardiology follow-up. If the chest pain changes—comes at rest, lasts longer, or doesn’t respond to nitro—go straight to the ER."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2136-02-28 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I understand. I’ll watch for that and keep my pills straight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2136-02-28 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. Take it easy today, and call us with any concerns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2136-02-28 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, doctor. I appreciate the care."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2136-02-27 18:25:00",
    "admission_end": "2136-02-28 16:45:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 65-year-old male with a history of coronary artery disease, prior CABG and stents, end-stage renal disease on hemodialysis, diabetes, and peripheral vascular disease was admitted for chest pain that occurred after a dialysis session and walking. The pain was typical of his stable angina, associated with back pain and nausea, and resolved with sublingual nitroglycerin. Evaluation showed no EKG changes and stable cardiac enzymes, ruling out acute myocardial infarction. He was managed with observation, continuation of anti-anginal therapy, and an increase in amlodipine from 2.5 mg to 5 mg daily. His insulin glargine dose was reduced to 6 units daily due to dialysis-related sensitivity. He remained pain-free, was discharged home the next day with instructions to monitor weight and continue medications, and advised to follow up with cardiology.",
